// ===================== 9.3 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('9.3  In return for'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ The test the partner applies is the one that produces the cycle: **is the return something the other side can actually give?** In about a third of first attempts it is not — the return is asked of a clerk and is a change of policy — and the sentence is wasted however well it is written.'));
out.push(A('**b** Names the exchange in one sentence: **2** and **4**. Not: **1** (two sentences — the reader can take the first and refuse the second), **3** (_might look at_ gives nothing) and **5** (nothing named on either side). ⚠ **1** is the one to read out, because it is the shape most students write and it looks perfectly reasonable on the page.'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — because a concession in one sentence and a request in another can be answered by taking the first and refusing the second; and Rim gets **a refusal code on every slip**, which she had asked for twice in eleven months without conceding anything.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** **Is it in their gift?** and **is it no bigger than what you conceded?** Both must pass.   **2** There is **no exchange at all**: a concession made in November is a gift by January, and the request in January is a new request.'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **The arithmetic is the teachable thing here and it should go on the board as two figures, not as a principle.** Mrs Shaaban conceded **a fault, in figures, about her own depot** — nine of forty consignments — and asked for **three characters on a form.** She had asked for the same three characters twice in eleven months and been ignored; conceding the nine, in the same sentence as the ask, produced an answer **in twelve days.** **A return plainly cheaper than the concession is very hard to refuse in writing**, and that sentence is the whole cycle. ⚠ The error to watch for is the opposite instinct: a student whose return is too large will make **the concession** bigger. **Make the return smaller instead** — it is almost always possible and it is always better.'));
out.push(A('**_On the understanding that_ is the shape to mark hardest, because it is the one that turns a concession into a threat without the writer intending it.** _On the understanding that the code appears by Thursday_ tells the reader what happens on Friday **without the writer having to say it** — ⚠ and a reader who hears a threat stops reading the argument, **so the writer has spent a real concession to buy an enemy.** The rule for this unit: **the shape may be used once, and never with a date in the same sentence.** If a date is genuinely needed, it goes in a separate sentence with a reason attached to it, and then it is a timetable and not a consequence.'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _(a fault of your own)_ _In return for a refusal code on every slip, I accept that nine of our forty consignments left here with the paperwork incomplete._ **(fault conceded; three characters asked)**',
  '**2** _(about a figure)_ _In return for the February release, I accept that my figure of twenty minutes was wrong and that the field costs about two hours._',
  '**3** _(three characters on a form)_ _In return for the letters PW, TM or QT in the box on the rejection slip, entered by whoever signs it, I accept that the late paperwork is ours._',
  '**4** _(the return is a name)_ _In return for being told who decides this, I accept the figure as the directorate has it._ ⚠ **Costs the reader nothing and cannot honestly be refused.**',
  '**5** _(if that is accepted, then)_ _If that is accepted — and I accept that February is nearly full — then I ask only that the specification be kept on file for the May release._',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ Mark these on **two things and in this order: is the return in their gift, and is it smaller than the concession?** Write _gift ✓/✗_ and _size ✓/✗_ in the margin and nothing else on a first pass. Item **4** is the one to read out and the one to come back to in 10.7 and 11.7: **when the return is in nobody\'s gift at your level, ask for the name of the person whose gift it is.** It is the only return in the book that cannot be refused without rudeness.'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**A** _(the concession)_ We accept that nine of our forty consignments left with the paperwork incomplete.',
  '**B** _(one sentence)_ In return for a refusal code on every slip, I accept that nine of our forty consignments left here with the paperwork incomplete and I shall date-stamp every consignment that leaves.',
  '**A** In their gift: **yes** — whoever signs the slip writes three characters. No bigger: **yes**, because a fault in figures is worth more than three characters.',
  '**B** _(with a name on the return)_ **A code in the box on the rejection slip, entered by whoever signs it.** ⚠ Shorter than the version without the name, which is what always happens.',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('The exchange sentence in each of the eight, and whether the return passed both tests. ⚠ **The gift test fails in about three of the eight** — the return is asked of somebody who cannot give it, usually a clerk asked for a change of policy. And the second finding, which is worth naming: **almost nobody puts a name or a post on the return until asked to, and the named version is always shorter.** Ask one pair to read both versions aloud and let the class hear the shortening.'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**a** _In return for one field with a date in it in the February release, I accept that the October release was full and that the field costs about two hours and not the twenty minutes I have been saying. **Shape: _in return for_**, because the return is the thing they can give and it belongs at the front where it will be read._',
  '**b** _**Pricing:** my concession is a corrected figure about my own request — six times larger than theirs. My return is two hours of somebody\'s time. ⚠ **The return is worth more than the concession**, so the return goes smaller: **one field, no interface, and I shall write the procedure note myself.** That is about forty minutes, and now the exchange balances._',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Eleven months of refused consignments with no reason on any slip, and Rim had conceded something on Tuesday: that nine of their forty consignments last quarter left with the paperwork incomplete, which was true, she had checked, and she had been implying it was four. The sentence she wrote was: in return for a refusal code on every slip, I accept that the late paperwork is ours and I shall date-stamp every consignment that leaves this depot. One sentence, and that is the whole lesson of the cycle — because a concession in one sentence and a request in another hands the reader a choice, which is to take the concession and refuse the request, and nobody does that deliberately: they do it because the two sentences arrived separately. Tareq asked about two months apart, and then there is no exchange at all: a concession made in November is a gift by January, the request in January is a new request, and the other side has forgotten that the concession ever cost anything. Omar asked what you can ask for in return, and there are two tests and both have to pass — is it in their gift, since a clerk cannot give you a change of policy, and is it no bigger than what you conceded, since a small concession and a large return is a trade dressed up and the reader prices it in four seconds. Rim thought hers was smaller than what she gave, and it was, and that is why it worked: she conceded a fault in figures about her own depot and asked for three characters on a form, and a return plainly cheaper than the concession is very hard to refuse in writing. Dima asked about the three phrases: in return for puts the return first and is the plainest, if that is accepted then puts the concession first for a reader who has to be brought along, and on the understanding that is the conditional form and the dangerous one — use it once and never with a deadline in the same sentence, or you have written a threat, because a deadline tells the reader what happens on Friday without your having to say it, and a reader who hears a threat stops reading the argument. Hiba asked whether the return needs a name, and it needs a name or a post always, because an unnamed return is refused by nobody and done by nobody. And the answer in Rim\'s case came in twelve days, with a code on every slip since — she had asked for it twice in eleven months, conceded nothing either time, and does not think either request was read.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the partner\'s exchange sentence and which of the two things is worth more. ⚠ Collect the pairs where the **return** was worth more and ask those students to shrink the return rather than inflate the concession. **Say why in one sentence**, because the instinct is strong and it does not go away on its own: **a concession inflated to balance an ask is a concession nobody believes.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** Concession in one sentence, request in the next. → **✓** one sentence. Two hands the reader a choice.   **✗** A return the reader cannot give. → **✓** ask the gift question first.   **✗** _On the understanding that the code appears by Thursday._ → **✓** no date in that sentence.   **✗** _A code on the slip._ → **✓** _entered by whoever signs the rejection._ An unnamed return is done by nobody.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** **In return for a refusal code on every slip, she accepts that the late paperwork is theirs and will date-stamp every consignment.** Her figures: **forty consignments, nine of them incomplete** — and she had been saying four.'));
out.push(A('**2** Because **thirty-one of the forty were not their fault** and she has no way of telling those thirty-one apart. **A depot that cannot see why a consignment was refused cannot stop it happening again** — and the nine she can now stop, because she knows about them.'));
out.push(A('**3** Not a change to the refusal policy _(not theirs to give)_, not a right of appeal, and **not an explanation of the thirty-one already refused** — because those are gone, **and asking for them would turn three characters into a quarrel.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
