// ===================== 9.4 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('9.4  Four words'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ Something is nearly always lost in the compression, and the stage is not asking students to pretend otherwise: **the question is whether what was lost mattered**, and that question is the content of the whole cycle. **Do not let the room answer "nothing was lost"** — it is almost never true and it stops the cycle before it starts.'));
out.push(A('**b** Concede inside the clause: **2**, **3** and **5** — _for all_ + noun phrase, _much as_ + first-person clause, and the inverted form. Not: **1** and **4**, which concede in sentences of their own. ⚠ **4** is the one to read out, because _it is expensive. That is true. But it pays._ is three sentences doing the work of four words, **and the reader has stopped at the second one.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — she cuts four paragraphs of concession to four words, _for all its cost_, and refuses to shorten **the strongest objection**, because a reader who holds it strongly must see it answered in a sentence of its own.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** _Would you be content for the reader to stop at the comma?_ — yes, four words; no, its own sentence.   **2** **_For all_ takes a noun phrase and _much as_ takes a first-person clause** — and _much as_ concedes **the writer\'s own feeling and not a fact**, which makes it the weakest of the four and the most often used dishonestly.'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **The comma question is the only thing from this cycle that has to survive the lesson, and it should be on the board in exactly these words: _would you be content for the reader to stop at the comma?_** It works because a subordinate clause **is** read as subordinate — _for all its cost_ tells the reader that the point has been dealt with and to keep going — **which is a service when it is true and a deception when it is not.** Mrs Khoury\'s description of being on the receiving end is the part to read aloud: **you put the page down agreeing with it, and two hours later the objection comes back with nothing attached.** ⚠ And the division of labour is the thing to mark on from here to the end of the unit: **four-word concessions in the other paragraphs, one full concession, once.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _(about cost)_ _For all its cost, the second pad pays for itself inside a quarter._ **(noun phrase)**',
  '**2** _(about delay)_ _For all the eleven days, the second survey does not depend on anybody\'s drawings._',
  '**3** _(a figure you do not dispute)_ _Much as I respect the October figure, it is a figure about a different quarter._ ⚠ **Weakest of the four: it concedes respect and concedes nothing about October.**',
  '**4** _(inverted)_ _Full though the release was, the specification had been written since 14 November._',
  '**5** _(one you refuse to shorten, and why)_ _The second pad will not help the eleven customers who order by telephone._ ⚠ **It stays in a sentence of its own, because it is the strongest thing anybody will say against the pad and a reader who holds it would stop at the comma and never meet it.**',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ Mark these on the comma question and on nothing else at first. Then mark the **shape**: _for all_ needs a noun phrase and students will write _for all it is expensive_; _much as_ needs a first-person clause and students will write _much as the figure is respected_, which concedes nobody\'s feeling at all. Item **5** is the one to collect from everybody — **one concession each, refused, with the reason** — and the reasons are what the class should hear, not the concessions.'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
