// ===================== 9.5 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('9.5  What did it cost the writer?'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ The second half of the instruction is the one that makes the cycle personal and it must not be skipped: **price the pair from the 9.1 homework.** The weak version will price at **nothing** — that is why it was comfortable to go on answering — and a student who discovers that about their own four years of argument has learnt the unit.'));
out.push(A('**b** Cost the writer something: **2** and **4** — a figure withdrawn, and a count corrected with _two of the nine I signed myself_ attached to it. Cost nothing: **1**, **3** and **5**. ⚠ **5** is the one to read out: _it is true that these matters are complex_ **concedes a property of the universe** and takes nothing from anybody.'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — **what did it cost the writer?** — and of twenty-two paragraphs that looked like concessions, **four cost the writer anything at all**; the other eighteen conceded things nobody had disputed.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** Conceding **the undisputed** · conceding **somebody else\'s position** · conceding **the irrelevant** · **conceding and reinstating** in the same sentence.   **2** They are **a signal and not a verdict**: Mr Barghouti wrote _granted that_ in 9.1 and it cost him four years of his own argument. **The question is whether a cost follows.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **Give the test its closed list or it becomes an opinion.** A concession costs **a position, a figure, an argument the writer had been using, or a request they wanted** — four things, and if none of the four can be named, the sentence was decoration **however true it is, and it usually is true.** A test with a closed list gets applied; a test that asks whether something "really" concedes gets argued about. ⚠ And teach the second question with its figure attached: _is the thing conceded something the other side actually said?_ — **half of all rhetorical concessions answer an objection nobody made**, and in Mrs Nasser\'s twenty-two it was eleven of the eighteen. **Go back and look** is the instruction; nobody does it unprompted.'));
out.push(NOTE('**Mrs Nasser priced her own paragraph first, before the twenty-two, and that is the order to use with your own class — including with your own example.** ⚠ **Two of her three concessions cost her nothing, and she had written the page believing it was a generous page.** **Everybody\'s first result is like that**, and the discomfort is the point: it is the only part of this unit that cannot be learnt by watching somebody else do it. Protect the kindness clause when you mark: ⚠ **a rhetorical concession is usually a habit and not a trick** — four kinds of sentence that sound like generosity and are in every manual of polite prose. **_This concedes something I never said_ is a sentence that gets answered. _This is dishonest_ is not.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
