out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Hiba read twenty-two paragraphs from her training group, all of them conceding something, and four of them were concessions. She applied one question to each and it took about four seconds a paragraph: what did it cost the writer? A real concession costs a position, a figure, an argument they had been using, or a request they wanted — and eighteen of the twenty-two cost nothing at all. Omar asked what the eighteen were conceding, and the answer is things nobody had disputed: of course no method suits every class, when nobody in the room had said that any method suits every class, so the writer has conceded a point that was never made and the paragraph looks generous and is empty. That is the first of four kinds, and they are worth having names for: conceding the undisputed; conceding somebody else\'s position, as in admittedly the directorate has been under pressure, which is not the writer\'s to concede and costs the directorate something and the writer nothing; conceding the irrelevant, which is a small adjacent fact given away so that the real objection appears to have been met, such as it is true that the form could be better laid out when the objection was about its length; and conceding and reinstating in the same sentence, which is 9.2 seen from the reading side. Dima asked whether the words themselves are the warning — of course, admittedly, it is true that — and they are a signal and not a verdict, which matters: Adnan wrote granted that in 9.1 and it cost him four years of his own argument, so the words are honest and the question is whether a cost follows them. Rim asked whether one question is enough, and there is a second, which catches the first kind: is the thing conceded something the other side actually said? Half of all rhetorical concessions answer an objection nobody made, and they are written in good faith by people who have never checked — which was the commonest fault in Hiba\'s twenty-two by a long way. Mazen asked what you do as a reader with a page like that, and you read it as a case and not as an argument: a page with three rhetorical concessions and no real one has conceded nothing, and you should expect it to be unmoved by anything you say, which is useful to know before you spend a week answering it. And Hiba said she applied the test to her own page first, before the twenty-two: two of her three concessions cost her nothing, and she had written the page believing it was a generous page.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the price of each of the partner\'s concessions, and where the two of you disagreed. ⚠ **Where there is a disagreement about a price, the writer is wrong** — and the rule has a reason: **the cost is a fact about what the writer can no longer say**, and a reader can see that more clearly than the writer can. Say the reason, not just the rule; otherwise the stage becomes an argument about authority.'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _Of course no system is perfect._ → **✓** price it: nothing. Nobody said it was.   **✗** _Admittedly the directorate has been under pressure._ → **✓** not yours to concede.   **✗** Conceding the layout when the objection was the length. → **✓** go back and read what was said.   **✗** _This is dishonest._ → **✓** _this concedes something I never said._ One gets answered.   **✗** Pricing everybody else\'s page and not your own. → **✓** yours first.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** **Her own** — _of course no method suits every class_ — and she starts there **because otherwise the note cannot be read**: a note that prices twenty-one other people and not the writer is read as marking and answered as tone.'));
out.push(A('**2** All four are **in the first person** and all four contain **a figure or a quotation**: a count corrected, the writer\'s own earlier sentence quoted and withdrawn, a request given away, and an objection named with _I have no answer to that_ **and a full stop.**'));
out.push(A('**3** Write the cost in the margin, and **if the margin says _nothing_, either find a real concession or take the paragraph out.** ⚠ Her reason is the one to keep: **a page that concedes nothing will not be answered, and being answered is the entire point of conceding at all.**'));
