// ===================== 9.6 =====================
out.push(h2('9.6  The paragraph that gives itself away first'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open, and **timed**. Twenty-five seconds, five sentences, and the order fixed. ⚠ **Nobody manages it on a first attempt and the first sentence is where they stop** — because it is somebody else\'s sentence and the room has not practised saying one aloud at full strength since 9.1. **Run it twice.** The second attempt is better in almost every pair.'));
out.push(A('**b** **2** gives the objection away first. **1** puts it last, after _however, some people say_, where it has nothing after it; **3** gives no objection at all and no position. ⚠ **1** is the one to read out, because it is the shape about a third of any examination class writes, **and it ends the essay on somebody else\'s case.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — in the **first** sentence of the paragraph, because an objection in the last sentence has nothing after it and the essay ends on somebody else\'s case; and the paragraph carries most of the **communicative achievement** and **organisation** marks and is where a candidate\'s **language** shows.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** The objection in its strongest form · the concession with a full stop · the limit, a different thing · the figure that survives · what follows from it.   **2** It is **the commonest closing move in the examination and the worst**: the objection has nothing after it, so the candidate has raised it and left it standing, **and a reader cannot tell whether that was courage or carelessness.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **This is the examination cycle and the arithmetic is the part to teach explicitly, because candidates lose the marks on proportion and not on English.** The task asks **140–190 words**; this book writes **180–220**, as it has since Unit 1. **Four paragraphs — your position · the first note · the concession paragraph · what follows — and the concession paragraph takes about a third of the words.** It is worth it because it is **the only paragraph in the essay where all four of the examiner\'s marks are visible at once**: content in the objection and the figure, communicative achievement in the concession, organisation in the fixed order, and language in the subordination and inversion that arrive naturally once the order is fixed. ⚠ **Sentence four is the one that fails** — the surviving figure — in five or six of eight attempts, because the objection and the limit have become easy by this point in the unit. **Mark sentence four first.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _(sending samples twice)_ _The strongest objection is that it doubles the laboratory\'s work for a fortnight. There is no answer to that; the fortnight is February. That does not touch the figure for the year. Of forty-one disputed results last year, thirty-one came from a single sample. So the fortnight buys thirty-one fewer disputes._',
  '**2** _(recording every decision)_ _The strongest objection is that five lines a meeting is twenty minutes a week nobody has. That is simply true. That does not touch the decisions nobody was at. Of the eleven queries last quarter, nine were about meetings the asker had not attended. So the twenty minutes answers nine queries._',
  '**3** _(a reason box on a form)_ _The strongest objection is that eleven people will fill it in badly. There is no answer to that. That does not touch the difference between blank and wrong. Four fields on the present form are filled in badly and all four are read as filled. So a blank reason box is the only honest field on the page._',
  '**4** _(paying for staff training)_ _The strongest objection is that the trained leave. Granted: three of our seven leavers last year were the trained ones. That does not touch the four who stayed. The four between them now cover every shift without an agency. So the cost of three leavers bought a rota._',
  '**5** _(a rule you have had to face up to)_ _The strongest objection to the pencil board is that it loses a name about once a fortnight. There is no answer to that and I have lost two. That does not touch the four changes a week. A printed sheet takes a day to reissue. So the board costs a name a fortnight and saves four reissues a week._',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ Mark these **sentence by sentence and in order**, and give the paragraph a mark out of five rather than a comment. Sentence **1**: is the objection in its strongest form, and is it first? Sentence **2**: is there a full stop and no _but_? Sentence **3**: is the limit a different thing? Sentence **4**: **is there a figure?** Sentence **5**: does anything follow, or is it a repetition of the position? **Sentence four is the one that will be missing**, and the marking order makes that visible to the student without an explanation.'));
