out.push(h2('9.7  Review & Mediate: Both sides have conceded'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready – Unit 9 quiz'));
out.push(A('**1** _**Granted** that the release was full, the field was already specified._   **2** _In return **for** a code on the slip, we accept the paperwork._   **3** _That does not **touch** the new decisions._   **4** _Much **as** I respect the figure, it is about February._   **5** _Her **insistence** on a second portion settled it._   **6** _He refused to **water** down the proposal._'));
out.push(A('⚠ Items **1** and **5** are the two that carry the unit. **1** looks trivial and is not: _granting that_ is real English and perfectly grammatical, **so the printed option was removed from the check** — but _granting_ shifts the concession into the writer\'s own act of allowing, where _granted that_ states it as settled, and the difference is audible. **5** is the word-formation item: _her insisting_ is also grammatical, which is why the check item uses _by sheer ___ instead, where only the noun will go. Item **6** is from the declared set — _water down_ — and the bare infinitive after _refused to_ is the whole of the test.'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** Open — the five months of correspondence, with the two buried lines underneath and their dates.'));
out.push(A('**b** Because **each side was reading for the other side\'s case and not for its concessions**: you look for what you have to answer, and a concession is the one part of a letter that needs no answer, **so the eye goes past it.** And the dispute has one question left: **who checks the seal, and against what?**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **This is the fifth mediation of the course and the fifth kind, and the class should be told the series, because it is the architecture of the book: 4.7 both parties had overclaimed; 6.7 the record was silent; 7.7 the document was defective; 8.7 both reviews were true and incomplete; 9.7 both sides have conceded and neither has read the other\'s concession.** The move to drill is the third: **put the two concessions in one sentence and read it aloud.** ⚠ Then protect the half of the cycle students will skip — **the test for whether the dispute survives.** Both of the Stage 3 disputes **do** survive it, and that is deliberate: **a surviving question is the normal result**, and it is smaller and better defined than what the parties had. About half the class will announce that there was never any disagreement, **which is the error the cycle exists to prevent**, and which turns an agreement back into a quarrel in one sentence.'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
